  <p class="lede"><strong>The Amityville Horror</strong> — 1979, remade 2005. Built on a house where a family was murdered.</p>

  <h2>The Fly on Screen</h2>
  <p>A priest named Delaney comes to the house to bless it. The family is outside and does not hear him, so he goes upstairs on his own to a second-floor room and begins.</p>
  <p>The room starts to fill with flies. It is out of season and there is no reason for them. The door swings shut behind him. He keeps going. The flies get worse until they are on his face and he does not brush them away.</p>
  <p>Then violent sickness takes him. The door swings open, the buzzing drops off, and a voice tells him to get out.</p>
  <p>He runs. He never warns the family. Every later attempt he makes is turned back — his phone calls break into static, his hand blisters on the receiver, his car nearly kills him on the road, and he ends up blind and broken.</p>

  <h2>What the Fly Is Doing</h2>
  <p>The flies are the doorkeeper. A man has come to speak words over a house, and the swarm stops the words. That is the entire scene: ritual against insect, and the insect wins.</p>
  <p>The picture is telling the audience that the house is stronger than the church, and it makes that argument by showing a collar, a formula, and a failure.</p>
  <p>But look at what never happens in that room. Nobody opens a book. Nobody names a commandment. Nobody in that house is asked to repent of anything. A man arrives with a memorized formula and expects the words themselves to do the work.</p>
  <p>The film reads the failure as proof of the devil's power. It is better read as proof that the formula was never Yahuah's to begin with.</p>
  <p>And the swarm does its usual announcing. A family was murdered in that house. Flies gather where something has died, and here they are telling the truth about the address while the ritual tells the audience a lie about authority.</p>

  <h2>The Name Behind It</h2>
  <p>Ahaziah, king of Israel, was badly hurt and sent men to ask Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron, whether he would live. Baal-zebub means lord of the fly. Elijah met the messengers on the road with a question and a sentence — is there not a God in Israel, and the king will not come down off that bed (2 Kings 1:2-4). He did not build the fly-god a temple. He asked him one question. That was enough. The same name is the title of the prince of devils in the gospels (Matthew 12:24).</p>
  <p>Note what the priest was doing when the flies came. He was inquiring of a house about an unseen power, with a form of words, on his own authority. Ahaziah inquired of the fly-god on his own authority too.</p>

  <h2>What Scripture Says</h2>
  <p>There is no house blessing in Torah. When a structure was under a plague, Yahuah gave a legal procedure — the priest came and inspected it, stones were pulled out, it was scraped, and if it returned the house was torn down and carried outside the city (Leviticus 14:33-45). Inspection, judgment, and a verdict. Not a man with a formula.</p>
  <p>Authority was never in the words. Some men tried using the name of Yahushua as a formula without belonging to Him, and the spirit answered that it knew Yahushua and knew Paul, but did not know them — and they ran out of that house naked and wounded (Acts 19:13-16). That is Delaney, in the first century, with the same result.</p>
  <p>The order is given plainly: submit yourselves to Yahuah, then resist the devil, and he will flee from you (James 4:7). Submission first. A man who has not submitted has nothing to resist with, no matter what he is wearing.</p>

  <h2>The Line</h2>
  <p><strong>The flies did not beat the Book. They beat a formula that was never in it.</strong></p>

